{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '215,290p'\"","aggregated_output":"   215\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   216\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   217\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   218\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   219\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   220\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   221\t\n   222\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   223\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   224\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   225\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   226\t\n   227\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   228\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   229\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   230\tfiling each protocol error under its id. Answers whose id is not\n   231\toutstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics.\n   232\tThis is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n   233\tgoes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   234\t\n   235\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   236\t\n   237\t- `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n   238\t  and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n   239\t  different reachability.\n   240\t- `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n   241\t  error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for\n   242\t  `a` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is `transport-error`\n   243\t  with `:dao.stream/retry? true`: the source's map, `invalid-anchor`\n   244\t  included, arrives later as an answer.\n   245\t- `next` `c`: a filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a\n   246\t  `next` request for `c` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is\n   247\t  `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle\n   248\t  yet.\n   249\t- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n   250\t  `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly `dao.stream.md`\n   251\t  Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream\n   252\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   253\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   254\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   255\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   256\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   257\t\n   258\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   259\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   260\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   261\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   262\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   263\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   264\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   265\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   266\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   267\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   268\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   269\tthe retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n   270\t\n   271\t**Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n   272\tprojection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n   273\tend (3.3), or the medium is gone. The link then abandons its outstanding\n   274\tids and reports each\n   275\tabandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n   276\tanswers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n   277\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n   278\t`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n   279\tthe channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n   280\tcaller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n   281\t\n   282\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   283\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   284\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   285\t\n   286\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n   287\tdescriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n   288\tprobe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n   289\t`#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n   290\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
